# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
unit1_scripts_l09_l10.py
High-volume 20-25 minute broadcast tiki-taka scripts for Lectures 09 and 10.
"""

SCRIPTS_L09 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 09 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora. Today, we launch into Section 1.5 of our course workbook, found on page 16: Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today represents a major milestone in your algebra journey. We are taking the fraction arithmetic skills we mastered back in Lectures 01 and 02 and merging them with the variable polynomials we have studied all week. A rational expression is simply a fraction where the numerator and denominator are polynomials—like 5 over w, or 3 over x squared. Students are often intimidated when variables appear downstairs in the denominator, but the fundamental law of fractions NEVER changes: to add or subtract pieces, they must share a common denominator!\nProf. Park: That is the universal principle: A/C + B/C = (A + B)/C, and A/C - B/C = (A - B)/C. If the denominators already match, you simply add or subtract the numerators and keep the common denominator. Think of physical units in everyday Montana life: imagine agricultural irrigation ditches in the Gallatin Valley. If you have one canal pumping 3 cubic feet of water per second through a diversion gate of capacity C, and a second canal feeding 2 cubic feet per second through that same gate capacity C, the combined flow rate is (3 + 2) = 5 cubic feet per second over that identical gate capacity C! You never add the gate capacities together to get 2C! You only add the amounts of water flowing through them.\nTA Sora: Exactly! But if the denominators are different—like one pump measured in cubic feet per second and another in gallons per minute—you cannot combine them directly. You must find the Least Common Denominator, or LCD. Finding the LCD for algebraic expressions follows a simple, foolproof two-step strategy that works every single time: Step 1: Find the numerical LCD for the integer coefficients using standard times tables. For example, for 6 and 4, the numerical LCD is 12. Step 2: For each variable that appears in any denominator, take that variable raised to its HIGHEST exponent present in any single denominator! For instance, if one denominator has x and another has x cubed, the LCD must contain x cubed. Once you have the LCD, you scale each fraction by multiplying top and bottom by whatever factors are missing, combine the numerators over the common denominator, and reduce to lowest terms. Let's see this in action on slide 2!\nTA Sora: In Gallatin Valley irrigation management, ditch riders control the flow of water to alfalfa fields and cattle pastures. If headgate A delivers flow at rate 5/(6x^2) and headgate B delivers flow at rate 3/(8x), you cannot simply add the 5 and 3 to get 8, and add the denominators to get 14x^3!\nProf. Park: That would be like adding two fractions with different units without converting them first! In physics and engineering, you must find a common standard unit before combining rates. In algebra, that common unit is the Least Common Denominator.",
    "2": "Prof. Park: Slide 2 presents Section 1.5 Examples 1A and 1B on workbook page 16: Part A: Find the sum 1/6 + 3/4. Part B: Find the sum 2/x + 5/x.\nTA Sora: Let's start with Part A: 1/6 + 3/4. This reviews our numerical fraction foundation from Lecture 01. The denominators are 6 and 4. Multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18... and multiples of 4 are 4, 8, 12... The Least Common Denominator is 12! Now build equivalent fractions by multiplying by 1 in disguise: For 1/6, multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (1 · 2) / (6 · 2) = 2/12. For 3/4, multiply top and bottom by 3/3: (3 · 3) / (4 · 3) = 9/12. Now that the denominators are identical, combine numerators: 2/12 + 9/12 = (2 + 9)/12 = 11/12! 11 is prime, so 11/12 is in simplest form.\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B right next to it: 2/x + 5/x. Notice what happened: the numerical denominators 6 and 4 were replaced by the variable x! Sora, how do students react when they see this?\nTA Sora: Many students freeze because of the letter x, but look closely: the denominators ALREADY MATCH! Both fractions have the exact same denominator, x. Therefore, no scaling is needed! We simply add the numerators directly over the shared denominator: (2 + 5) / x = 7/x! That is all there is to it: 7/x.\nProf. Park: Let's highlight a very common mistake here: some students write 7 / 2x, mistakenly adding x + x in the denominator! Sora, remind everyone why that is mathematically impossible.\nTA Sora: The denominator tells you the size of the container! If you have 2 fifths of an apple and you add 5 fifths of an apple, you have 7 fifths—you do not have 7 tenths! In 1/12 + 9/12, the denominator stayed 12—it didn't become 24. In the exact same way, 2/x + 5/x stays over x, giving 7/x. Denominators never add together when combining fractions.\nTA Sora: Let us review the golden arithmetic rule: A/C + B/C = (A + B)/C. Notice that the denominator C is the denominator of the answer!\nProf. Park: If you have quarters, 1 quarter plus 2 quarters is 3 quarters, not 3 eighths! Denominators name the fraction unit; numerators count the units.",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Now let's advance to slide 3 with Examples 2A and 2B on page 16: Monomial Common Denominators: Part A: 5/w - 7/w^2. Part B: 3/x + 4/y.\nTA Sora: Let's dissect Part A: 5/w - 7/w^2. Here the denominators are different: one is w, and the other is w^2! Remember our golden rule for variable LCDs: take each variable to its HIGHEST power present in any denominator! The powers of w are w^1 and w^2. The highest power is w^2, so our LCD is w^2!\nProf. Park: Exactly. Notice that the second fraction, 7/w^2, already has the complete LCD. It needs no modification at all! The first fraction is 5/w. What factor is missing from its denominator to make it w^2? It is missing a single factor of w! So we multiply top and bottom by w/w: (5 · w) / (w · w) = 5w / w^2.\nTA Sora: Now both fractions share the common denominator w^2! Subtract the numerators: (5w - 7) / w^2. Can we simplify 5w - 7 any further? 5w and -7 are unlike terms, so they cannot be combined. And can we cancel the w in 5w with the w^2 in the denominator? ABSOLUTELY NOT!\nProf. Park: That is one of the most critical warnings in the entire course! Why can't we cancel that w, Sora?\nTA Sora: Because addition and subtraction act as industrial cement! 5w is glued to -7 by subtraction. You can only cancel common FACTORS that multiply an entire numerator, never individual TERMS inside a sum or difference! Unless you can factor a w out of both 5w and -7, you cannot touch it. So (5w - 7) / w^2 is our final, fully simplified answer.\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B: 3/x + 4/y. Here we have two completely different variables in the denominators: x and y! Since they share no common factors, our LCD is simply their product: xy. Scale the first fraction by y/y: (3 · y) / (x · y) = 3y / xy. Scale the second fraction by x/x: (4 · x) / (y · x) = 4x / xy. Now combine numerators over the common denominator: (3y + 4x) / xy (or (4x + 3y) / xy). Clean, elegant, and fully simplified!\nProf. Park: Let us review why we take the HIGHEST power of each variable when finding the LCD:\nTA Sora: Think of packing luggage for a camping trip in Yellowstone: if one person brings a 2-person tent (x^2) and another brings a 5-person tent (x^5), your car trunk (the LCD) must be big enough to fit the 5-person tent! If it fits the 5-person tent, it easily fits the 2-person tent with space to spare. That is why the LCD always takes the highest exponent of every variable factor!",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Let's move to slide 4 with Examples 2C and 2D: Two-Variable Denominators: Part C: 3/y + 5/(2y). Part D: 4/(3a) - 1/(2a).\nTA Sora: Let's analyze Part C: 3/y + 5/(2y). Look at the denominators: y and 2y. Numerically, we have coefficients 1 and 2, so the numerical LCD is 2. For the variable part, both have y to the first power, so the variable LCD is y. Combining them gives our Least Common Denominator: 2y!\nProf. Park: Notice that the second fraction 5/(2y) already has the denominator 2y. The first fraction is 3/y, which is missing a factor of 2. So we scale 3/y by multiplying top and bottom by 2/2: (3 · 2) / (y · 2) = 6 / (2y). Now add the numerators: 6/(2y) + 5/(2y) = (6 + 5) / (2y) = 11 / (2y)! 11 is prime and shares no factors with 2, so 11/(2y) is our final answer.\nTA Sora: Now let's tackle Part D: 4/(3a) - 1/(2a). Look at the denominators: 3a and 2a. Let's break this into our two modular steps: Step 1: The numerical coefficients are 3 and 2. Multiples of 3 are 3, 6, 9... and multiples of 2 are 2, 4, 6... The numerical LCD is 6! Step 2: Both denominators contain the variable a to the first power, so the variable part is simply a. Combining them gives an LCD of 6a!\nProf. Park: Now let's build our equivalent fractions: To turn 3a into 6a, we need to multiply by 2. So multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (4 · 2) / (3a · 2) = 8 / (6a). To turn 2a into 6a, we need to multiply by 3. So multiply top and bottom by 3/3: (1 · 3) / (2a · 3) = 3 / (6a). Now subtract the numerators: (8 - 3) / (6a) = 5 / (6a)! 5 and 6 share no common factors, so 5/(6a) is in lowest terms. Notice how treating the numbers and the variables as separate modular components makes finding the LCD completely stress-free.\nProf. Park: Let us highlight the algebraic malpractice warning again: why does (9x + 20) / (24x^2) NOT simplify by cancelling x?\nTA Sora: Let us test it with a simple number! Suppose x = 2.\nThen the numerator is 9(2) + 20 = 18 + 20 = 38.\nThe denominator is 24(2^2) = 24(4) = 96.\n38/96 reduces to 19/48.\nIf a student illegally cancelled the x, they would get (9 + 20)/(24 · 2) = 29/48, which is completely different from 19/48!\nNumbers never lie. You can only cancel common FACTORS that multiply the entire numerator and denominator, never terms attached by plus or minus!",
    "5": "Prof. Park: To conclude Lecture 09, let's examine slide 5 with Examples 2E and 2F: Mixed Constant and Variable Denominators: Part E: 2/(7r) + 3/(14r). Part F: 5/(6k^2) - 1/(4k).\nTA Sora: In Part E, look at the denominators: 7r and 14r. Notice that 14 is a direct multiple of 7 (7 · 2 = 14)! And both have r to the first power. Therefore, our LCD is simply 14r! The second fraction 3/(14r) stays unchanged. We only need to scale the first fraction: multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (2 · 2) / (7r · 2) = 4 / (14r). Now add: 4/(14r) + 3/(14r) = (4 + 3) / (14r) = 7 / (14r).\nProf. Park: But wait! Look at our result: 7 / (14r). Are we finished? TA Sora: Absolutely not! Always inspect your final fraction for reduction opportunities! 7 on top and 14 on the bottom both divide by 7! 7 divided by 7 is 1. 14 divided by 7 is 2. So 7/(14r) reduces cleanly to 1 / (2r)! Remember: the 1 in the numerator is mandatory. You cannot write 2r—the 2r is in the denominator, so it must be 1/(2r).\nProf. Park: Now let's conquer Part F: 5/(6k^2) - 1/(4k). This is our grand finale for Lecture 09! Coefficients: 6 and 4. Multiples of 6 are 6, 12... multiples of 4 are 4, 8, 12... The numerical LCD is 12! Variables: k^2 and k^1. The highest power is k^2. So our total LCD is 12k^2!\nTA Sora: Let's scale both fractions to 12k^2: For 5/(6k^2), multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (5 · 2) / (6k^2 · 2) = 10 / (12k^2). For 1/(4k), to turn 4k into 12k^2, we must multiply by 3k! Multiply top and bottom by 3k / 3k: (1 · 3k) / (4k · 3k) = 3k / (12k^2). Now subtract the numerators: (10 - 3k) / (12k^2). Can we reduce 10 - 3k over 12k^2? No, 10 and 3k share no common factor, so nothing can be factored out. Our final answer is: (10 - 3k) / (12k^2)!\nProf. Park: In Lecture 10, we continue Section 1.5 with advanced rational expressions: higher powers, three-term sums, and binomial numerators. See you all in Lecture 10!\nTA Sora: Notice the beauty of our final fraction in Example 3: (21b - 4a^2) / (30 a^3 b^2).\nCould we cancel the b in 21b with b^2 in the denominator?\nProf. Park: No! Because the term -4a^2 does not have a b! If a factor is not shared by EVERY term in the numerator, it cannot be cancelled!\nTA Sora: That is the ironclad rule of rational expressions: all terms in the numerator must share the factor before anything can cancel across the fraction bar!"
}

SCRIPTS_L10 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 10 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora. Today, we take our rational expression skills to the master level in Section 1.5 Part 2, found on workbook page 17: Advanced Rational Addition and Subtraction.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today's lecture addresses the problems that students find most challenging in Unit 1: expressions with higher-degree variable denominators like x^4, three fractions added together simultaneously, and fractions with entire binomials in their numerators. When an entire binomial is in a numerator, subtracting fractions requires distributing a negative sign across multiple terms. If you master today's six examples, you will be completely prepared for every rational expression on your exams.\nProf. Park: That is right, Sora. In engineering and physical science, compound rate equations—like calculating the equivalent electrical resistance of multiple parallel circuits, 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3, or computing shared work rates when multiple construction crews pave Montana highways—rely directly on combining three or more rational terms. For instance, if Crew A takes x hours to pave a section of Highway 191 near Big Sky, and Crew B takes 2x hours, their combined hourly paving rate is 1/x + 1/(2x) = 3/(2x)! Let's look at slide 2 to begin with Example 3A!\nTA Sora: Culvert hydraulics under mountain passes like Bozeman Pass or Reynolds Pass along the Madison River deal with turbulent water flow during spring snowmelt. If an engineer models outflow rates with composite rational expressions, every single minus sign represents back-pressure or friction loss.\nProf. Park: Misplacing a single minus sign turns a subtraction into an addition, predicting double drainage when in fact water is backing up! That is why civil engineers and algebra students alike treat binomial numerators with absolute structural respect.",
    "2": "Prof. Park: Slide 2 presents Section 1.5 Example 3A on workbook page 17: Add and simplify: 3 / (7x^2) + 5 / (14x).\nTA Sora: Let's find our Least Common Denominator systematically: Look at the numerical coefficients: 7 and 14. 14 is a multiple of 7, so our numerical LCD is 14. Look at the variable powers: x^2 and x^1. The highest power is x^2. Combine them: our LCD is 14x^2!\nProf. Park: Now let's check what each fraction needs: The first fraction is 3 / (7x^2). Its denominator already has x^2, but needs its coefficient scaled from 7 to 14. So we multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (3 · 2) / (7x^2 · 2) = 6 / (14x^2). The second fraction is 5 / (14x). Its coefficient is already 14, but it only has x to the first power. It needs one more x to reach x^2! So we multiply top and bottom by x/x: (5 · x) / (14x · x) = 5x / (14x^2).\nTA Sora: Now both fractions share the identical denominator 14x^2. Combine the numerators: 6/(14x^2) + 5x/(14x^2) = (6 + 5x) / (14x^2), which we write in standard order as (5x + 6) / (14x^2). Can we cancel x in 5x with x^2 on the bottom? Never! 5x is part of the sum (5x + 6). Unless the entire binomial has a common factor with the denominator, no canceling is allowed. (5x + 6) / (14x^2) is our final answer!\nTA Sora: Here is my personal study tip for workbook page 17: whenever you see a problem like (A + B)/C - (D + E)/C, draw bright red parentheses around (D + E) before you write another stroke of your pencil!\nProf. Park: That single second of drawing protective parentheses eliminates 90% of all student errors on rational expression exams. Notice also why factoring out the greatest common factor in the numerator is essential: in (4x - 4)/(4x), 4 is a common factor of BOTH terms upstairs. Factoring gives 4(x - 1). That allows the 4 to cancel with the 4 downstairs. If a student simply crosses out the 4x in the front, they leave -4 behind with no denominator, which is an algebraic catastrophe!\nTA Sora: Always factor before you cancel! No factoring, no cancelling! That is our golden motto!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Let's elevate the challenge with Example 3B on slide 3: a three-term rational expression with a 4th power: Simplify: 2/x - 9 / (10x^4) + 3 / (5x^2).\nTA Sora: Three fractions at once! Don't let the length intimidate you—our LCD strategy works identically regardless of how many fractions there are. Look at the denominators: x, 10x^4, and 5x^2. Step 1: Numerical coefficients are 1, 10, and 5. Multiples of 10 and 5 give a numerical LCD of 10! Step 2: Variable powers are x^1, x^4, and x^2. The highest exponent present is 4! So our variable LCD is x^4. Combining them gives our overall Least Common Denominator: 10x^4!\nProf. Park: Now let's scale each of the three fractions into equivalent fractions with denominator 10x^4: Fraction 1: 2/x. To turn x into 10x^4, multiply by 10x^3: (2 · 10x^3) / (x · 10x^3) = 20x^3 / (10x^4). Fraction 2: 9 / (10x^4). Its denominator is already 10x^4! It needs no scaling at all. Fraction 3: 3 / (5x^2). To turn 5x^2 into 10x^4, multiply by 2x^2: (3 · 2x^2) / (5x^2 · 2x^2) = 6x^2 / (10x^4).\nTA Sora: Now combine all three numerators over the common denominator 10x^4, paying close attention to the operations: The first operation is subtraction, and the second is addition: (20x^3 - 9 + 6x^2) / (10x^4). Rearrange the numerator terms in descending degree order: (20x^3 + 6x^2 - 9) / (10x^4). None of the numerator terms can be combined because their degrees are 3, 2, and 0. And 20, 6, and -9 share no common factor with 10. Our three-term rational expression is fully simplified!\nProf. Park: Look closely at how we simplified (3x + 6) / (3x^2) on slide 3:\nTA Sora: Notice that we factored out 3 from BOTH terms in the numerator: 3(x + 2).\nBecause 3 is multiplied by (x + 2), 3 is a FACTOR!\nAnd 3 in the denominator is also a FACTOR!\nNow—and ONLY now—can the 3 cancel top and bottom, leaving (x + 2) / (x^2)!\nFactoring first is the only legal ticket to cancelling in rational expressions!",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Moving to slide 4, let's examine Examples 3C and 3D: Multi-Term Common Denominators: Part C: 2 / (3b) + 1/4 + 1 / (6b). Sora, walk us through finding the LCD when one term is a pure constant fraction without a variable!\nTA Sora: That is a wonderful detail to notice! The denominators are 3b, 4, and 6b. First, look at the numbers: 3, 4, and 6. Multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18... 12 is divisible by 3, 4, and 6! So our numerical LCD is 12. Second, look at the variable: b appears to the first power in two denominators. Even though the middle fraction 1/4 has no b, any fraction in the family that needs b must receive b! So our variable LCD is b. Combining them gives an LCD of 12b!\nProf. Park: Let's scale all three fractions to 12b: Fraction 1: 2 / (3b). Multiply top and bottom by 4/4: (2 · 4) / (3b · 4) = 8 / (12b). Fraction 2: 1/4. To turn 4 into 12b, we must multiply by 3b! Multiply top and bottom by 3b / 3b: (1 · 3b) / (4 · 3b) = 3b / (12b). Fraction 3: 1 / (6b). Multiply top and bottom by 2/2: (1 · 2) / (6b · 2) = 2 / (12b).\nTA Sora: Now combine the numerators over 12b: 8 + 3b + 2 = (3b + 10) / (12b). Can we reduce 3b + 10 over 12b? Look at 3, 10, and 12: 3 and 12 share 3, but 10 is not divisible by 3. 10 and 12 share 2, but 3 is not divisible by 2. There is NO common factor shared by all terms. Therefore, (3b + 10) / (12b) is in lowest terms!\nProf. Park: On slide 4, look at the distribution of scaling factors: when multiplying (x + 3) by 3, you must distribute 3 to BOTH x and 3: 3x + 9!\nTA Sora: And when multiplying (2x - 1) by 2, you must distribute 2 to BOTH 2x and -1: 4x - 2!\nNever leave a term behind when building equivalent fractions upstairs!\nProf. Park: In Example 3C, notice the factored form of the final answer: 7(x + 1) / (12x). On exams, both 7(x + 1)/(12x) and (7x + 7)/(12x) are 100% acceptable. Factoring out the 7 lets you quickly check whether anything can cancel with the denominator 12x. Since 7 shares no common factor with 12, and (x + 1) does not cancel with x, you know the fraction is irreducible!\nTA Sora: That is why factoring your final answer gives you an instant built-in correctness check!",
    "5": "Prof. Park: Now we arrive at slide 5, which features the single most critical problem type in Section 1.5: Example 3E: Subtracting Fractions with Binomial Numerators: (2x - 5) / (3x) - (5x + 9) / (6x).\nTA Sora: Sound the emergency siren! This is where hundreds of algebra students lose points on Exam 1! Why? Because of that minus sign between the fractions! When you subtract a fraction whose numerator is a binomial, that minus sign MUST distribute to EVERY term in the numerator! Sora's Golden Rule: Immediately wrap every binomial numerator in protective parentheses before you do anything else: [ (2x - 5) ] / (3x) - [ (5x + 9) ] / (6x)!\nProf. Park: Let's find the LCD first. The denominators are 3x and 6x. 6 is a multiple of 3, and both have x to the first power. So our Least Common Denominator is 6x! The second fraction already has 6x. For the first fraction, we must multiply top and bottom by 2/2: 2 · (2x - 5) / (3x · 2) = (4x - 10) / (6x).\nTA Sora: Now place both numerators over the common denominator 6x: [ (4x - 10) - (5x + 9) ] / (6x). Look at that second set of parentheses: - (5x + 9). Distribute the minus sign: -5x - 9! So our numerator becomes: 4x - 10 - 5x - 9. Now combine like terms: 4x - 5x = -x. And -10 - 9 = -19! So our numerator is -x - 19. Over the common denominator 6x, our final answer is: (-x - 19) / (6x) (or - (x + 19) / (6x)). If a student failed to distribute the negative, they would get -10 + 9 = -1, which is completely wrong. Protective parentheses save the day!\nProf. Park: Let us review the test value check on slide 5: why is plugging in x = 1 such an incredible strategy?\nTA Sora: On an exam, you don't have an answer key in the back of the book. But by spending 30 seconds plugging in x = 1 into both the original problem and your simplified answer, if the two values match, you know with 100% mathematical certainty that your answer is correct! It gives you total peace of mind before turning in your exam paper.\nProf. Park: Remember that the negative sign applies to the ENTIRE quantity (5x + 9), so both signs flip: -5x - 9! That gives -10 - 9 = -19. If you got -1, you forgot to distribute the negative sign! Always double-check every sign distribution!",
    "6": "Prof. Park: To conclude Lecture 10, let's look at slide 6: Example 3F on workbook page 17—our Second Binomial Subtraction Master Problem: (3y + 7) / (8y) - (9y - 11) / (4y).\nTA Sora: Let's apply our bulletproof procedure: Step 1: Wrap the binomial numerators in protective parentheses: [ (3y + 7) ] / (8y) - [ (9y - 11) ] / (4y). Step 2: Find the LCD. The denominators are 8y and 4y. 8 is a multiple of 4, so our LCD is 8y! Step 3: Scale the second fraction. To turn 4y into 8y, multiply top and bottom by 2/2: 2 · (9y - 11) / (4y · 2) = (18y - 22) / (8y).\nProf. Park: Now write over the single common denominator 8y: [ (3y + 7) - (18y - 22) ] / (8y). Distribute the negative sign to the second numerator: - (18y - 22) becomes -18y + 22! Notice that -(-22) became POSITIVE 22!\nTA Sora: That double negative turning into +22 is where the magic happens! Now combine like terms in the numerator: 3y - 18y = -15y. +7 + 22 = +29! So our numerator is -15y + 29 (or 29 - 15y). Over the common denominator 8y, our final simplified answer is: (-15y + 29) / (8y)! Can we cancel the y in 15y with the y in 8y? No! -15y is tied to 29 by addition. No canceling is possible.\nProf. Park: That brings us to the conclusion of Lecture 10 and the complete mastery of Section 1.5! In Lecture 11, we will begin Section 1.6 on workbook page 18: Solving Linear Equations. Outstanding job everyone, and we will see you in Lecture 11!\nTA Sora: Let us review the full 4-step checklist for adding and subtracting rational expressions:\n1. Wrap all binomial numerators in protective parentheses!\n2. Find the LCD of all denominators (LCM of numbers, highest power of variables).\n3. Scale each fraction top and bottom by its missing factors.\n4. Distribute and combine like terms over the single LCD, and factor to check for final simplification!\nProf. Park: With that checklist, you are fully equipped for any rational expression problem. We look forward to seeing you in Lecture 11 for linear equations!"
}
